This guide generates the local files Trellis needs and runs the Rust Trellis runtime from the installed CLI, which auto-manages a local nats-server binary. No containers or external NATS server are required.

By the end, you will have:

- a local folder at `~/trellis` with generated Trellis and NATS files
- a managed nats-server process running on your machine
- the current Rust Trellis runtime running at `http://localhost:3000`
- runtime health and readiness checks that return version and mode metadata
- Console running at `http://localhost:5173` for UI development

## What you need

- Git
- [Deno](https://deno.com) v2+
- the `trellis` CLI installed (see [Install the Trellis CLI](/guides/install-trellis-cli))
- network access on first run: the CLI downloads a pinned, checksum-verified
  nats-server binary and caches it under `TRELLIS_CACHE_DIR` (default
  `~/.cache/trellis` on Linux) for later runs; every run cryptographically
  revalidates the cached archive and binary against the pin, reinstalling
  corrupted or replaced cache entries

The config init command generates NATS operator, account, credential, signing-key, auth-callout, resolver, and authorization-trust files natively. The authorization root seed remains offline in the generated bundle; runtime config references only the online context-issuer seed.

## 1. Generate the local bundle

```sh
trellis init config --out ~/trellis
```

This creates a movable init bundle under `~/trellis`:

| Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `nats/` | NATS config, resolver data, service credentials, and signing seeds |
| `trellis/config.toml` | Trellis runtime config using relative file paths |
| `trellis/data/` | Local Trellis SQLite data directory |
| `trellis/auth/` | Pinned root, issuer manifest/certificate, and online issuer seed |
| `session.seed` | Private 32-byte event-session seed referenced as `../session.seed` from `trellis/config.toml` |
| `trust/authorization-root.seed` | Offline root seed for issuer rotation; never mount this into the runtime |

The generated Trellis config omits `nats.jetstream.replicas`. On startup,
Trellis probes NATS JetStream topology and uses `1` for this standalone local
server. Production deployments can set `nats.jetstream.replicas` explicitly, or
omit it to let Trellis choose `3` only when enough current JetStream metadata
peers are visible.

Useful local flags:

| Flag | Default | Use when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `--name Trellis` | `Trellis` | You want a different Trellis display name; the NATS server name is derived from this unless overridden |
| `--server-name trellis` | derived from `--name` | You need a specific NATS server name |
| `--public-origin http://localhost:3000` | `http://localhost:3000` | Trellis is exposed on a different local origin |
| `--nats-server-url nats://127.0.0.1:4222` | `nats://127.0.0.1:4222` | Server-side clients reach NATS somewhere else |
| `--nats-websocket-url ws://localhost:8080` | `ws://localhost:8080` | Browser clients reach NATS somewhere else |

## 2. Check out the source tree

```sh
TRELLIS_VERSION="$(trellis --version)"
TRELLIS_VERSION="${TRELLIS_VERSION#trellis }"
TRELLIS_VERSION="${TRELLIS_VERSION%%+*}"
git clone --branch "v${TRELLIS_VERSION#v}" --depth 1 https://github.com/qlever-llc/trellis.git
cd trellis
```

This checks out the release tag that matches your installed CLI. You only need
the checkout for Console development and source-level work; `trellis server`
runs the runtime from the installed CLI. If you already have a checkout, use
that directory instead.

## 3. Start Trellis

`trellis server` runs the runtime in-process and auto-manages a local
nats-server when no `--nats` URL is given:

```sh
trellis server all --config "$HOME/trellis/trellis/config.toml"
```

Keep this terminal running. On first run the CLI downloads the pinned
nats-server release (v2.14.4), verifies its checksum, and caches it under
`TRELLIS_CACHE_DIR` (default `~/.cache/trellis`). It renders a host-path NATS
config from the generated bundle (`nats/nats.conf` plus `nats/jwt.conf`),
spawns the server, and stops it when `trellis server` exits. The managed server
listens on the bundle's loopback ports:

| Port | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `4222` | Native NATS clients |
| `8080` | Browser websocket clients |
| `8222` | NATS monitoring and health |

The runtime safely converges the streams and KV buckets owned by its selected
mode during startup, exposes the embedded portal, first-admin and login flows,
authorization trust/context registries, `/healthz`, and `/readyz`. Startup
fails closed if configured trust cannot be verified or published. On a fresh
bundle, the first full run boots into first-administrator onboarding and prints
a bootstrap URL for the first admin; run
`trellis server all --config "$HOME/trellis/trellis/config.toml" --check` before
any run to see which stores the first run will create.

The initial administrator remains the permanent bootstrap administrator. To
change its username and password, start Trellis with `--reset-admin`. Trellis
prints a replacement one-time URL, preserves the account and administration
authority, and revokes its existing sessions when the reset is completed. The
command targets this bootstrap account even when additional administrators
exist.

### External NATS (production pattern)

To run against an external NATS server instead of a managed one, pass its URL:

```sh
trellis server all --config "$HOME/trellis/trellis/config.toml" \
  --nats nats://127.0.0.1:4222
```

With `--nats`, `trellis server` behaves exactly like the `trellis-server`
binary. Permanent deployments use the quadlet units under `deploy/quadlet`,
which run their own external NATS container plus `trellis-server`; that path is
unchanged.

### Pre-installed nats-server binary

`--nats-binary <path>` manages an existing nats-server binary instead of
 downloading and caching one: no cache directory is used. It is an explicit
 trusted-operator escape hatch — the binary is NOT re-verified against the
 Trellis pin or version. The path is canonicalized and must be a regular
 executable owned by root or the current user, with no group/world-writable bits
 on the file or any parent directory.

### Single-container deployment (runtime OCI image)

The runtime OCI image ships the `trellis` CLI and the pinned nats-server
(v2.14.4) baked in at `/usr/local/bin/nats-server` — downloaded and
checksum-verified at image build time against `conformance/nats-binaries.json`,
never at container runtime; `nsc` is not in the image because the Rust bootstrap
material is generated natively. The image's `ENTRYPOINT` remains
`trellis-server` for the external-NATS/quadlet deployment path (unchanged). For
a single container with a managed nats-server, override the entrypoint, mount
the bundle read-only, and keep the mutable state on a writable volume:

```sh
docker volume create trellis-bundle
docker volume create trellis-state
# Generate the bundle into the state volume once (as the image's non-root user):
docker run --rm --user 10001:10001 --entrypoint trellis \
  --volume trellis-state:/var/lib/trellis ghcr.io/qlever-llc/trellis:latest \
  init config --out /var/lib/trellis/bundle
# Rewrite the bundle's SQLite store paths to the state volume (mounted at
# /var/lib/trellis) so the bundle volume can be mounted entirely read-only:
docker run --rm --user 10001:10001 --entrypoint sed \
  --volume trellis-state:/var/lib/trellis ghcr.io/qlever-llc/trellis:latest \
  -i 's|\./data/|/var/lib/trellis/data/|g' /var/lib/trellis/bundle/trellis/config.toml
# Prepare the writable state volume for the image's uid: its root is
# root-owned on some runtimes, and the runtime must create its SQLite stores
# and NATS state there:
docker run --rm --user root --entrypoint sh \
  --volume trellis-state:/var/lib/trellis ghcr.io/qlever-llc/trellis:latest \
  -c 'mkdir -p /var/lib/trellis/data && chown -R 10001:10001 /var/lib/trellis'
# Copy the edited bundle into its own named volume:
docker run --rm --user root --entrypoint cp \
  --volume trellis-state:/from:ro --volume trellis-bundle:/to ghcr.io/qlever-llc/trellis:latest \
  -a /from/bundle/. /to/
# The bundle's creds/secrets are 0o600: the run container uid must own the
# bundle volume contents to read them:
docker run --rm --user root --entrypoint chown \
  --volume trellis-bundle:/to ghcr.io/qlever-llc/trellis:latest \
  -R 10001:10001 /to
# Run: read-only rootfs, read-only bundle volume, writable state volume; managed
# NATS state in /var/lib/trellis/nats via --nats-state-dir:
docker run --rm --name trellis --read-only --tmpfs /tmp \
  --user 10001:10001 --publish 3000:3000 \
  --volume trellis-bundle:/etc/trellis:ro,Z \
  --volume trellis-state:/var/lib/trellis:rw,Z \
  --entrypoint trellis ghcr.io/qlever-llc/trellis:latest \
  server all --config /etc/trellis/trellis/config.toml \
    --nats-binary /usr/local/bin/nats-server \
    --nats-state-dir /var/lib/trellis/nats
```

The bundle (`/etc/trellis`) is mounted read-only from its own named volume: the
runtime reads its config, credentials, and trust material from it. All mutable
NATS files (`nats.local.conf`, `jwt.local.conf`, pid file, resolver JWTs,
JetStream data) are written under `/var/lib/trellis/nats`, and the runtime's
SQLite databases land in `/var/lib/trellis/data` because the copied bundle's
config points their paths at the state volume. Quadlet and
other external-NATS deployments are unchanged: they keep running
`trellis-server` against their own NATS container.

## 4. Check the converged runtime

In another terminal, run the public read-only preflight:

```sh
trellis check --config "$HOME/trellis/trellis/config.toml"
```

This validates the complete TOML, all configured NATS credentials and
connectivity, required streams and KV buckets, migration compatibility on
temporary copies made without opening configured SQLite paths, the
root-to-issuer trust chain, and the file/SQLite/registry trust floors without
mutating production state or opening listeners. Missing required databases,
streams, buckets, or trust records fail the check. `--mode` can select one split
runtime mode; the default `all` checks the deduplicated union.

## 5. Check runtime readiness

Query both runtime endpoints:

```sh
curl http://localhost:3000/healthz
curl http://localhost:3000/readyz
```

Each response should include fields like `version` and `mode`.

## 6. Start the Console

In another terminal from the source checkout:

```sh
cd ts/apps/console
cp .env.example .env
deno task dev
```

Open `http://localhost:5173` for Console development. It authenticates against
the Rust `trellis-server`, which owns the browser login, OIDC, bootstrap, portal,
authorization-context, and well-known trust routes. There is no separate
TypeScript control-plane runtime.

## 7. Verify the local stack

```sh
curl http://127.0.0.1:8222/healthz
curl http://localhost:3000/healthz
curl http://localhost:3000/readyz
curl -I http://localhost:5173/
```

At this point you have:

- NATS running as a managed process started by `trellis server`
- Trellis running from the installed CLI with `trellis server`
- Rust runtime health and readiness checks returning version and mode metadata
- Console running from source for UI development
